{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Epic close REFUSED, correctly, and not forced.\n\nVerdict: stale-material — the epic plan-review attestation pinned 14 children and the epic now has 16. That is accurate: two children were added DURING execution, both for good reasons and both recorded at the time.\n- eagleeyed-commutable-mantisray (bc66), the _cli/_registry.py validation split, filed when F11 turned out to need Route headroom that a file sitting at exactly 800 lines could not give it.\n- florid-cookable-fly (90cb), filed when the plan-review gate caught a seventh consumer of the plan-review-exempt vocabulary that the F3 plan had missed.\n\nSo the epic genuinely is not the epic that was reviewed, and the gate is right to say so. The named remedy is to re-run review-plan on the epic and retry the close; that is running now. No force override was used, and none will be.\n\nOne detail from the refusal worth keeping, because it settles a question I raised earlier in this session: the gate states explicitly that ticking an AC checkbox is attestation-SAFE — box state is normalized out of the material fingerprint, so a tick is never the cause of staleness. My earlier observation that ticking left the verdict unchanged was right, and now it is confirmed by the implementation rather than by inference.\n\nEvery one of the 16 children is closed and all 14 epic changes are MERGED. The epic close is the only step left."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788421021378458001,"uuid":"465c3218-9f94-4508-87af-64c1632a4383"}